  ok   selfcheck: with kyverno unreachable this script exits 3 and its last line is SKIP:
-- pooled kyverno apply is not the admission verdict --
   pooled exit code across all 3 policies: 1 (FAIL)
   real admission outcome (Audit never refuses): admitted
   CONFIRMED: pooled exit code disagrees with the real admission outcome.
   A minor bump (new Audit policy) is invisible to admitted/refused, but a naive
   engine reading only kyverno apply's $? across the whole set would call this a
   narrowing (wrongly major) -- it must read validationActions per policy instead.

== 1.0.0 -> 2.0.0 ==
   require-department-label: major  (expected major, OK)  -- no-department: admitted -> REFUSED
   WHOLE-BODY BUMP: major  (ticket's asserted answer: major, OK)

== 2.0.1 -> 2.1.1 ==
   require-department-label: none  (expected none, OK)  -- no fixture in the corpus moved
   require-known-department-label: patch  (expected patch, OK)  -- legal-department: refused -> admitted
   require-owner-annotation: minor  (expected minor, OK)  -- added, Audit-only -- cannot refuse anything
   WHOLE-BODY BUMP: minor  (ticket's asserted answer: minor, OK)

== honest finding ==
Per-NAMED-POLICY, all three of the ticket's asserted bumps rederive exactly:
  require-department-label 1.0.0->2.0.0:        major (Audit->Deny promotion)
  require-known-department-label 2.0.1->2.1.1:  patch (enum widened +legal)
  require-owner-annotation absent->2.1.1:       minor (added, Audit-only)

But CONTEXT.md scopes semver at the WHOLE-BODY level ('a policy version covers
the whole body'), and at that level the real v2.0.1->v2.1.1 release bundles the
minor addition AND the patch widening into ONE tag. Combining rules say the more
significant change wins, so the whole-body-correct bump for that release is MINOR,
not the 'patch' label the ticket's own bullet points attach to '2.1.1' -- that
bullet describes require-known-department-label's own delta in isolation, not the
release. The real tag's decimal (2.1.1, minor bumped 0->1 but patch held at 1
instead of resetting to 0) does not follow textbook bump-and-reset semver either
way -- CONTEXT.md defines per-change classification but is silent on reset-on-bump,
so this is named as a real gap in the historical release line, not glossed over.

Minor cannot be rederived from bare admission pass/fail alone: a brand-new Audit
policy produces ZERO admitted/refused transitions for any fixture by construction
(Audit never refuses), so there is no verdict movement to observe -- it can only be
detected by a STRUCTURAL diff (a policy name present in NEW, absent in OLD) plus its
validationActions, never by watching the pass/fail move on a fixed corpus.
